## What happened

.NET 11 removed the DSA implementation on macOS. Apple's native crypto framework (Security.framework / CryptoKit) never supported DSA, so earlier .NET versions shimmed it through a bundled or system-installed OpenSSL. In .NET 11 that shim was dropped, and `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** legacy signatures (not generate new ones), the cleanest path is to use a managed DSA implementation that doesn't depend on the OS crypto stack:

### Option 1 – BouncyCastle (recommended for legacy verify-only)

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Signers;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var publicKey = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var signer = new DsaSigner();
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);

    // hash the message yourself or use SignerUtilities for the full hash+verify:
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

### Option 2 – Conditional runtime guard (if you still target all three OSes with the built-in API)

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
}
else
{
    // fall back to BouncyCastle or reject gracefully
}
```

### Migration checklist

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| 1 | Add `BouncyCastle.Cryptography` NuGet package |
| 2 | Replace `System.Security.Cryptography.DSA` verify calls with BouncyCastle's managed signer |
| 3 | Suppress the `SYSLIB0058` obsoletion warning on any remaining DSA references (or remove them) |
| 4 | Long-term: re-sign artifacts with ECDSA or RSA and retire DSA entirely |

This keeps your library portable across all three platforms without relying on OS-level DSA support.
